I'm part of one of those groups, unfortunately working in different
languages (for TAEB, a NetHack bot written in Perl, we use
Term::VT102l: <http://search.cpan.org/~ajwood/Term-VT102-0.91/
VT102.pm>; and in a ttyrec player I'm writing, written in Java, I use
the terminal emulator from JTA (although it needed to be modified
somewhat heavily to work in my application): <http://javatelnet.org>).
Although I haven't written such a program in C, other people have; you
could try looking at IPBT <http://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/
ipbt/>, a ttyrec player written in C that parses the frames itself,
using the terminal emulator from PuTTY. Similar libraries should
probably be available in most established programming languages by
now; Go is relatively new, so I wouldn't be surprised if nobody had
written a terminal emulator for it yet. Try searching for telnet/ssh
clients when looking for terminal emulator libraries; it's very common
(although not universal) for them to either include one or be bundled
with one.

